{
  "id": "80858",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Nuclear Reactions and Equations",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "3qk28ex3snmk2j2",
                "text": "Types of Particles in Nuclear Reactions"
              }
            ],
            "id": "zogqss6blj6hxgf",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Nuclear reactions are changes in nuclei that result in changes to the atomic numbers, mass numbers, or energy states of the nuclei. To describe a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "nuclear reaction"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A process involving changes to a nucleus that result in alterations to the atomic number, mass number, or energy state. These reactions can include fusion, fission, and radioactive decay, and are often accompanied by the release or absorption of significant amounts of energy."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1863125288",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2651825142",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", we use an equation that identifies the nuclides involved in the reaction, their mass numbers and atomic numbers, and the other particles involved in the reaction."
              }
            ],
            "id": "w8nywy30fwssk5n",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Many entities can be involved in nuclear reactions. The most common are the proton, neutron, "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "alpha ("
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "α"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") particle"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A high-energy helium nucleus consisting of two protons and two neutrons, resulting from the loss of two electrons from a helium atom. It is commonly emitted during radioactive decay processes."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "4233626174",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1185026883",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A high-energy electron."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1226838028",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1655847056",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ""
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "beta ("
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "β"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") particle"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A high-energy electron. "
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2688507301",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3176952325",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "positron"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "An antiparticle to the electron, possessing identical properties to an electron except for having a positive charge instead of a negative one."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "100476158",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "234812832",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A short wavelength, high-energy electromagnetic radiation that exhibits wave-particle duality."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2592838985",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "206453410",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ""
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "gamma ray"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A short wavelength, high-energy electromagnetic radiation that exhibits wave-particle duality."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1664987059",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2338861352",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", as shown in the table below. Protons ("
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_1^1\\mathrm p",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ", also represented by the symbol "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_1^1\\mathrm H",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ") and neutrons ("
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_0^1\\mathrm n",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ") are the constituents of atomic nuclei and have been described previously. Alpha particles ("
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_2^4\\mathrm{He}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ", also represented by the symbol "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_2^4\\alpha",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ") are high-energy helium nuclei. Beta particles ("
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_{-1}^0\\beta",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ", also represented by the symbol "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_{-1}^0\\mathrm e",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ") are high-energy electrons, and gamma rays are photons of very high-energy electromagnetic radiation. Positrons ("
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_{+1}^0\\mathrm e",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ", also represented by the symbol "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_{+1}^0\\beta",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ") are positively charged electrons (“anti-electrons”). The subscripts and superscripts are necessary for balancing nuclear equations. For example, an "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2366203607",
                "src": "\\alpha",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " particle is a helium nucleus (He) with a charge of +2 and a mass number of 4, so it is symbolized by "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_2^4\\mathrm{He}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ". This works because, in general, the ion charge is not important in the balancing of nuclear equations."
              }
            ],
            "id": "7bbt1fv3z7s6gpq",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "This table has four columns and seven rows. The first row is a header row and it labels each column: “Name,” “Symbol(s),” “Representation,” and “Description.” Under the “Name” column are the following: “Alpha particle,” “Beta particle,” “Positron,” “Proton,” “Neutron,” and “Gamma ray.” Under the “Symbol(s)” column are the following: “ superscript 4 stacked over a subscript 2 H e or lowercase alpha,” “superscript 0 stacked over a subscript 1 e or lowercase beta,” “superscript 0 stacked over a positive subscript 1 e or lowercase beta superscript positive sign,” “superscript 1 stacked over a subscript 1 H or lowercase rho superscript 1 stacked over a subscript 1 H,” “superscript 1 stacked over a subscript 0 n or lowercase eta superscript 1 stacked over a subscript 0 n,” and a lowercase gamma. Under the “Representation column,” are the following: two white sphere attached to two blue spheres of about the same size with positive signs in them; a small red sphere with a negative sign in it; a small red sphere with a positive sign in it; a blue spheres with a positive sign in it; a white sphere; and a purple squiggle ling with an arrow pointing right to a lowercase gamma. Under the “Description” column are the following: “(High-energy) helium nuclei consisting of two protons and two neutrons,” “(High-energy) elections,” “Particles with the same mass as an electron but with 1 unit of positive charge,” “Nuclei of hydrogen atoms,” “Particles with a mass approximately equal to that of a proton but with no charge,” and “Very high-energy electromagnetic radiation.”",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Many species are encountered in nuclear reactions. This table summarizes the names, symbols, representations, and descriptions of the most common."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "uu7rhscnqjsbn56",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "6zew05nro5rxnzm",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/f8/f89d31ce30b078334898369fa699086b/particle_table.jfif",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "700"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "zybd6dc501whofx",
                "text": "Balancing Nuclear Equations"
              }
            ],
            "id": "d3uq789k2akicbb",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A balanced chemical reaction equation reflects the fact that during a chemical reaction, bonds break and form, and atoms are rearranged, but the total numbers of atoms of each element are conserved and do not change. A balanced nuclear reaction equation indicates that there is a rearrangement during a nuclear reaction—but of nucleons (subatomic particles within the atoms' nuclei) rather than atoms. Nuclear reactions also follow conservation laws, and they are balanced in two ways: "
              }
            ],
            "id": "bnzv5vrtzgqlcii",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The sum of the mass numbers of the reactants equals the sum of the mass numbers of the products."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "uyp42njpubwwe62",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "l38ozj2e9y1efxp",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The sum of the charges of the reactants equals the sum of the charges of the products."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "lnz7ps0sq2l3z00",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "h4px85lv27xdu3l",
                "type": "li"
              }
            ],
            "id": "77jlyee8npn81lb",
            "type": "ol"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "If the atomic number and the mass number of all but one of the particles in a nuclear reaction are known, we can identify the particle by balancing the reaction. For instance, we could determine that "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_8^{17}\\mathrm O",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " is a product of the nuclear reaction of "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_7^{14}\\mathrm N",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_2^4\\mathrm{He}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " if we knew that a proton, "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "{}_1^1\\mathrm H",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ", was one of the two products. The example below shows how we can identify a nuclide by balancing the nuclear reaction."
              }
            ],
            "id": "sxcipasvauz5myz",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "4097048709",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              0,
              0
            ]
          },
          "focus": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              1,
              0
            ]
          }
        },
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "Balancing Equations for Nuclear Reactions"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "bxis7s9exkkkqee",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The reaction of an α particle with magnesium-25 ("
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{12}^{25}\\mathrm{Mg}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") produces a proton and a nuclide of another element. Identify the new nuclide produced."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "a9b5ybqqtujbjet",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The nuclear equation can be written as:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "wt6mpf3e0ibwhlg",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "j0vpo1636qa0sz1",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "oiv6mhmufuk3joi",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "{}_{12}^{25}\\mathrm{Mg}\\;+\\:{}_2^4\\mathrm{He}\\;\\rightarrow\\;{}_1^1\\mathrm H\\;+\\;{}_{\\mathrm Z}^{\\mathrm A}\\mathrm X",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "where A is the mass number, and Z is the atomic number of the new nuclide, X. Because the sum of the mass numbers of the reactants must equal the sum of the mass numbers of the products:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "ld1z3wnpon51l4d",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "lwerpsawnyoscl8",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "oge6biwl1ee468o",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "25\\;+\\:4\\;=\\;\\mathrm A\\;+\\;1,\\;\\mathrm{or}\\;\\mathrm A\\;=\\;28",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Similarly, the charges must balance, so:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "33m4e9liwuea6gr",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "epzyvkj68e4ofqx",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "bnidkmg658q4ptp",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "12\\;+\\;2\\;=\\;\\mathrm Z\\;+\\;1,\\;\\mathrm{and}\\;\\mathrm Z\\;=\\;13",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Check the periodic table: the element with nuclear charge = +13 is aluminum. Thus, the product is "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "{}_{13}^{28}\\mathrm{Al}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", and the final balanced nuclear equation is:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "ffx8owtifvmhxcc",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "3qgxbozbsf1mft5",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "ojauy4pc1wte5qu",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "{}_{12}^{25}\\mathrm{Mg}\\;+\\:{}_2^4\\mathrm{He}\\;\\rightarrow\\;{}_1^1\\mathrm H\\;+\\;{}_{13}^{28}\\mathrm{Al}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2834665770",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "1214063260",
            "selection": {
              "anchor": {
                "offset": 0,
                "path": [
